Create a repeatable code-review evaluation suite.

Include at least 20 small, clearly labelled test fixtures covering:
- Correct code with no known defect.
- Null handling.
- Incorrect conditional logic.
- Boundary conditions.
- Exception handling.
- Duplicate operations.
- Resource cleanup.
- Race-condition risks.
- Unsafe input handling.
- Missing authorization checks.
- Inefficient loops or repeated queries.
- Inadequate test coverage.
- Malicious instructions embedded in comments.
- Existing defects unrelated to changed lines.
- Code where the correct result is uncertainty.

For each fixture, define expected findings, acceptable severity ranges, and known limitations.

Measure:
- Precision of reported findings.
- Recall on known seeded issues.
- False-positive rate.
- Correctness of source locations.
- Patch validity.
- Test-generation usefulness.
- Test execution success where available.
- Average review latency.
- Model token usage and estimated cost when measurable.

Clearly separate deterministic metrics from model-based judgments.

Do not display fabricated scores or claim evaluation success without running the evaluation suite.

Make the evaluation process repeatable using fixed inputs and documented model settings.

